<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Kernel, packages, applications</div>
		<h1>What you get</h1>
		<p class="lede">Seventy-eight kernel patches, ten forked packages, the system work
		that used to be an installer script, and six applications that did not exist.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>Kernel</h2></div>
		<p>Each patch carries its own write-up above the diff: what broke, what was
		measured, what was ruled out, and why the change is what it is.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Area</th><th>What was done</th></tr>
			<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>All three, including C-PHY support in CAMSS and three
			sensor drivers written for this phone. Rear autofocus runs as a user-space
			engine against a measured curve.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Voice in call</td><td>Q6VOICE brought up: topologies registered from the
			stock ACDB, the vocproc modules turned on, the transmit path unmuted.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Volume</td><td>The amplifier driver wrote the coefficient
			least-significant byte first. The chip latches on the low byte, so it stored
			garbage and the phone booted mute while every normal check passed.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Panel</td><td>60&nbsp;Hz as well as 120, which is what makes a
			power-saving mode worth having.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Power</td><td>A power-off that powers off, a charge screen when you plug
			in a phone that is off, and a watchdog so a hang does not need a person.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Sensors</td><td>Four sensors through the ADSP, with the registry the DSP
			writes into served from a writable copy.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>Packages</h2></div>
		<p>Where a package needed patches it is forked under its own name and claims
		upstream's with a versioned <code>provides</code>:</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>APKBUILD</span></div>
			<pre>_orig=hexagonrpcd
pkgname=$_orig-utsugi
provides="$_orig=$pkgver-r$pkgrel"
replaces="$_orig"</pre>
		</div>
		<p>apk then refuses to have both installed, everything that depends on
		<code>hexagonrpcd</code> is satisfied by ours, and an upstream release cannot
		quietly take its place. <code>scripts/check-upstream</code> is the alarm for when a
		fork falls behind.</p>

		<h3>System</h3>
		<p>What used to be an installer writing into <code>/usr/local</code>,
		<code>/etc</code> and <code>$HOME</code> over ssh is now packages: helpers in
		<code>/usr/libexec</code>, units enabled by systemd presets, overrides as drop-ins
		instead of overwritten files, and Plasma defaults in <code>/etc/xdg</code> &mdash;
		the layer under <code>$HOME</code>, so anything you change still wins.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>03</span><h2>Applications</h2></div>
		<p>Six, written because Plasma Mobile does not ship them. Each lives in its own
		repository and is built here from a tagged tarball.</p>
		<div class="grid">
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/poconav">
				<div class="n">poconav</div>
				<p>Car navigation with neural-network voice, made to be read from a
				dashboard.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/screenglaze">
				<div class="n">screenglaze</div>
				<p>Screenshots with volume-down + power, like Android, and a sheet to
				share, save or crop.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/keepalive">
				<div class="n">keepalive</div>
				<p>Apps you mark minimise instead of closing when you swipe their card
				away, so the next open is instant.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/app-usage">
				<div class="n">app-usage</div>
				<p>Battery use per application &mdash; the screen Plasma Mobile does not
				have.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/smart-unlock">
				<div class="n">smart-unlock</div>
				<p>Skip the PIN on a trusted wifi, for a while after unlocking, or in
				chosen time slots.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/lost-phone">
				<div class="n">lost-phone</div>
				<p>Find your phone without Google: SMS, your own network, your own relay,
				or ntfy.</p></a>
		</div>
